1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,360
of the most funstones like ever.

2
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:02,860
Of course, for Hitler, there's some of the eight

3
00:00:02,860 --> 00:00:06,320
documented cases where people came pretty close to killing him.

4
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:12,680
Because do ridiculous actions build kind of a god image?

5
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:13,960
Yeah, possibly.

6
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:16,160
So maybe that's what's going on.

7
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:19,800
Maybe it's just that once you are that powerful dictator,

8
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:21,320
you have a lot of wiggle room.

9
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:23,960
And you can do something that is actually kind of foolish,

10
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:26,760
but the odds that it actually leads to disaster for you

11
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:29,600
is so low because you have so many other safeguards.

12
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:31,880
So still, you might say, look, don't make enemies

13
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:34,160
for no reason at all but for friends.

14
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:36,480
That seems like pretty sensible advice,

15
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:37,920
regardless of what's going on.

16
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:42,800
All right, so anyway, so there is this intuition

17
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:44,880
that repudiation does make things work better

18
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:48,000
for all people, at least if all of them are participating.

19
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,040
So how can you rationalize a standard intuition?

20
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:52,400
So here, probably the best answer

21
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:56,840
is that if you go and add on free entry and exit.

22
00:00:56,840 --> 00:01:00,040
So if you add on free entry and exit, then repudiation

23
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:03,560
has the usual effects, or they were the expected effects.

24
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:07,280
So free entry, free exit.

25
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:08,880
That makes a big difference.

26
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:11,360
All right, so then, if there's free entry and exit,

27
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:13,280
and there's someone who has a bad reputation,

28
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:16,400
what is everybody going to do?

29
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,040
Leave, go away.

30
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:20,880
So if there's only one place on Earth

31
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:23,160
that you can buy food from, they might deliberately

32
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:25,840
want to build up a reputation for we give you,

33
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:29,000
we can charge exorbitant prices, we have crummy quality,

34
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,440
and if you don't like it, we will cut you off from our list

35
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:32,840
and you'll starve to death.

36
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:34,840
That were the only place on Earth.

37
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:38,120
But on the other hand, if there's

38
00:01:38,120 --> 00:01:40,800
other possibilities out there that you can constantly

39
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:42,640
easily switch to, then of course,

40
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:45,160
if you have that reputation, then you'll have zero customers.

41
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:48,440
And that problem will solve itself.

42
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:50,680
Again, of course, if, say, cities

43
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:53,280
that are threatened by Genghis Khan could just pick themselves

44
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:55,280
up and move 1,000 miles away, then again,

45
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:58,200
Genghis Khan would not be doing anything

46
00:01:58,200 --> 00:01:59,760
that would work out well for him.

47
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:01,600
It would just be pretty pointless.

48
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:03,220
Now, Gordon Tullock, as far as I know,

49
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:05,000
only did one experiment his whole career,

50
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,160
but it was a brilliant experiment, one of the best

51
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:08,200
ones I've ever heard of.

52
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:10,160
So what Tullock did is this.

53
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:12,640
He re-ran the standard prisoner of slum experiment

54
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:14,880
where you get paired up with a partner and play,

55
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:17,800
but he added on one difference from the rule,

56
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:21,760
which is you're allowed to change your partner if you want.

57
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:24,440
And the expected result happened.

58
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:26,640
First of all, hardly anyone actually

59
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:32,480
would betray anyone if they knew people could switch,

60
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:33,200
for two reasons.

61
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:34,640
So one is, if you did get betrayed,

62
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:36,240
then you would almost always switch.

63
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:38,120
Like, why should I stay with you after you're

64
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:39,120
stabbed me in the back?

65
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:41,960
And then secondly, once you have betrayed someone,

66
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:44,300
who are the only people that are willing to have anything

67
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:46,440
to do with you?

68
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:48,000
Other betrayers.

69
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,040
And it seemed like players figured this out

70
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:50,680
pretty quickly.

71
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:53,560
So even people who were inclined to betray would say, oh, wait.

72
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:54,400
I've got to go.

73
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:56,920
If I betray, not only am I going to lose my partner,

74
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:59,640
I'm going to be thrown into this crummy,

75
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:02,480
you know, this crummy, lupine proletariat of people

76
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:05,280
who no one else wants to have anything to do with.

77
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:06,880
I'll be ostracized.

78
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:08,120
I'll be an outcast.

79
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:10,840
And then I'll only be able to deal with other people like me.

80
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:12,520
People like me are terrible.

81
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:14,800
So I don't want that.

82
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:17,040
So Tullock set this up in this experiment.

83
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:19,720
This was a hypothesis, and it worked great.

84
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:21,880
So really a pretty amazing experiment,

85
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:26,160
showing that you could get near total cooperation just

86
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:28,480
by making it known that if you were not

87
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:30,840
happy with the result, you could go and fire your partner.

88
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:34,640
You may have had this in mind any time

89
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:36,680
that you were stuck on a class assignment

90
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:39,720
and the teacher just forcibly crammed people together.

91
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:41,920
And then, of course, there's always a bunch of people

92
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:42,840
who don't do any work, and then they

93
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:44,500
get shoved off onto people who actually

94
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:46,000
want to get a good grade.

95
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:48,720
You may have noticed this occurring at some point.

96
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:51,360
But on the other hand, if you were free to form your groups,

97
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:53,280
then it probably wouldn't happen.

98
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:56,560
So my kids told me the usual thing.

99
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:58,160
Teachers actually, if you suggest,

100
00:03:58,160 --> 00:03:59,720
will let people form their own groups.

101
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:00,440
They'll say, well, then it'll just

102
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:02,440
be some groups of hardworking kids

103
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:05,240
and other groups of lazy kids, and we don't want that.

104
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:07,160
Isn't that how it ought to be?

105
00:04:07,160 --> 00:04:09,560
Isn't that a well-functioning system

106
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:12,160
where people who don't do work are stuck with other people who

107
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:13,240
don't do work?

108
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:15,440
And if you want to turn over a new leaf,

109
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:18,880
you have to go and prove yourself and pay your dues

110
00:04:18,880 --> 00:04:23,080
and earn forgiveness for your past laziness.

111
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:24,440
No, that's not a good system.

112
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:26,200
A good system is where every good kid

113
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:27,600
is shackled to some freeloaders.

114
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,320
All right.

115
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:34,400
God, I hate class projects so much.

116
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:37,720
The model of absolute injustice.

117
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:38,640
So questions.

118
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:40,840
Questions about the Talk experiment,

119
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:42,240
reputation in general.

120
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:44,040
And again, if you do want to work on anything

121
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:46,840
involving reputation, you are already in the right place.

122
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:50,360
So Dan Klein's a great person to talk to.

123
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:54,240
Anyone who's doing experiments is a great person to talk to.

124
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:57,080
So I guess the main issue is trying

125
00:04:57,080 --> 00:05:00,880
to go and get credit for doing something not obvious.

126
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:03,200
Because of course, you can't write a paper on something

127
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:05,120
obvious is the problem.

128
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:07,280
Dan Klein wrote about it before it happened,

129
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:09,120
which I think made it less obvious.

130
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:11,040
So there's that.

131
00:05:11,040 --> 00:05:13,040
But again, I know he's got a bunch of other ideas

132
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:14,760
about how reputation can be expended.

133
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:18,480
So there's probably plenty of other things to do with it.

134
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:18,980
All right.

135
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:20,560
So questions about that.

136
00:05:20,560 --> 00:05:22,880
We have had some GMU students who've done dissertations

137
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:25,000
on the dark web and things like that.

138
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,800
And how can you have reputation with anonymity?

139
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:31,680
Which at first glance sounds impossible.

140
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:36,360
So my student, Julia Norgaard, had one on the dark web.

141
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:40,400
And again, I have to say, I've never actually been personally

142
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:41,880
on the dark web.

143
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:46,920
But her story basically was that even though the identity

144
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:49,360
of a human being is anonymous, but still there

145
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:52,760
is a reputation of this anonymous entity.

146
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:55,680
So there's like the anonymous store number one.

147
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:57,400
And you know that you have previously

148
00:05:57,400 --> 00:05:59,520
had good relations with anonymous store number one.

149
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:03,740
And so that is where you get the benefit.

150
00:06:03,740 --> 00:06:05,780
So I do remember that when I was in grad school,

151
00:06:05,780 --> 00:06:08,560
I had a bunch of arguments over email with my econ one teacher.

152
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:09,760
I said, well, I'm a graduate.

153
00:06:09,760 --> 00:06:11,200
I said, my undergraduate econ one teacher

154
00:06:11,200 --> 00:06:12,960
had a bunch of arguments with him over email.

155
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:14,120
Well, I kept saying the reputation

156
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:15,360
to do way more things than he thought.

157
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,840
And he kept saying that it was just libertarian fantasy.

158
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:21,600
And then in the early 2000s, he spontaneously

159
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:24,760
remembered the conversation, remembered

160
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:28,040
our respective positions, and set out his right.

161
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:31,240
Which is like being struck by lightning, right?

162
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:33,160
So normally the best you can hope for

163
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:34,600
is that someone changed their mind

164
00:06:34,600 --> 00:06:38,280
without remembering the users of credit for it.

165
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:41,440
And he said, look, I just thought that the whole asymmetric

166
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:43,000
information problem would kill this.

167
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,280
I never believed online commerce could take off.

168
00:06:45,280 --> 00:06:47,240
And now it has.

169
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:50,840
And if you think about what one kind of business

170
00:06:50,840 --> 00:06:53,120
has been absolutely instrumental to the internet working

171
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:55,760
as it has, so like has anyone ever bought anything

172
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:58,840
with cash over the internet ever?

173
00:06:58,840 --> 00:06:59,640
You never use cash.

174
00:06:59,640 --> 00:07:02,280
So what has been the key institution that has

175
00:07:02,280 --> 00:07:05,040
allowed the system to flourish?

176
00:07:05,040 --> 00:07:05,540
Credit.

177
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:06,320
Credit cards.

178
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:07,640
Credit cards are crucial.

179
00:07:07,640 --> 00:07:10,920
Even PayPal ultimately comes back to credit cards, right?

180
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:13,320
So credit cards have been crucial.

181
00:07:13,320 --> 00:07:15,400
If you think about how much the modern world owes

182
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:18,720
to credit cards, not just all e-commerce,

183
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:23,240
but like rental cars, and like hotels,

184
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:25,640
and just go down the list of all the things

185
00:07:25,640 --> 00:07:27,520
you can do with a credit card.

186
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:29,000
Mail order, right?

187
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,160
And again, what credit cards are all about,

188
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:33,160
I mean, even in the name credit card,

189
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:35,440
what does the word credit suggest?

190
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:36,080
You're reliable.

191
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:37,760
Yeah, yes, we're reliable.

192
00:07:37,760 --> 00:07:39,520
We have a reputation.

193
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:43,040
So you have a reputation as someone that repays his debts.

194
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:45,240
And pretty amazing given that it's essentially

195
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:47,740
possible for you to rack up an enormous amount of credit card

196
00:07:47,740 --> 00:07:49,280
debt and then just declare bankruptcy

197
00:07:49,280 --> 00:07:51,160
and not have to pay it.

198
00:07:51,160 --> 00:07:55,200
So fun fact is that this is doable.

199
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:57,680
It's also illegal to go and sign a contract waiving

200
00:07:57,680 --> 00:07:59,200
your right to bankruptcy.

201
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:00,720
So imagine what kind of a great deal

202
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:02,520
you would get from credit card companies

203
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:06,060
if you would just waive that right.

204
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:07,560
So questions about reputation?

205
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:08,720
Everyone happy?

206
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:10,040
Inspired?

207
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:10,540
Great.

208
00:08:10,540 --> 00:08:11,040
All right.

209
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:12,920
So let's all take out our credit cards

210
00:08:12,920 --> 00:08:17,360
and say a silent prayer of thanks for all that we owe them

211
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:19,320
and all that they have done for us,

212
00:08:19,320 --> 00:08:22,560
and how rarely anyone has ever written a poem

213
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:26,720
to the noble credit card, how rarely anyone has ever held it

214
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:28,760
up and cried a tear.

215
00:08:28,760 --> 00:08:30,320
Said, it was you.

216
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:33,600
I love you and all you've done for us.

217
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:36,440
There had never been a motion in the United Nations saying,

218
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:39,600
let us all have a day of honor for credit cards.

219
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:42,200
But should there be?

220
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:45,120
Can't we boldly remember the real heroes, credit card

221
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:45,620
companies?

222
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:48,000
All right.

223
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:49,000
All right.

224
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:50,280
So then moving on.

225
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:53,760
All right, so now we got monopoly and congestibility.

226
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:54,520
All right.

227
00:08:54,520 --> 00:08:57,480
So you've all seen the standard monopoly model at some point,

228
00:08:57,480 --> 00:08:58,360
right?

229
00:08:58,360 --> 00:09:00,160
Standard monopoly model.

230
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:03,040
So what does the monopolist do?

231
00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:04,080
You all know the answer.

232
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:08,320
What the monopolist does is they maximize PQ minus TC.

233
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:12,800
All right, so price times quantity minus total cost.

234
00:09:12,800 --> 00:09:15,000
And when they do so, they wind up

235
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,640
setting marginal revenue equal to marginal cost.

236
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:20,400
So if this is demand curve, then this

237
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:22,400
will be your marginal revenue curve.

238
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:25,120
And if this is your marginal cost curve,

239
00:09:25,120 --> 00:09:28,440
then monopolists, of course, have we seen 1,000 times,

240
00:09:28,440 --> 00:09:30,880
just go to the intersection of marginal cost and marginal

241
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:34,360
revenue, and then you move up to the demand curve.

242
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:37,000
And then that tells you what they will do.

243
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,480
So you've all seen what monopolists do before.

244
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:41,400
OK.

245
00:09:41,400 --> 00:09:45,480
Now, does this make sense in game theoretic terms?

246
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:48,480
Well, sure, this is one equilibrium.

247
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:50,320
It's totally one equilibrium.

248
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:52,600
But there's another equilibrium for you.

249
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:55,280
How about an equilibrium where consumers refuse to pay anything

250
00:09:55,280 --> 00:09:57,320
more than marginal cost, and then monopolists

251
00:09:57,320 --> 00:09:59,680
set price at marginal cost?

252
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:02,240
If this is what everybody believes is going to happen,

253
00:10:02,240 --> 00:10:03,840
does anyone have a reason to switch?

254
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:06,160
If the monopolist believes that no one will buy anything

255
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:08,360
at more than marginal cost, it has no reason

256
00:10:08,360 --> 00:10:10,040
to charge more than marginal cost.

257
00:10:10,040 --> 00:10:15,520
And if consumers refuse to pay anything

258
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:17,760
more than marginal cost, and they're

259
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:20,260
getting at a marginal cost, do they have any reason to offer

260
00:10:20,260 --> 00:10:20,760
more?

261
00:10:20,760 --> 00:10:21,520
No.

262
00:10:21,520 --> 00:10:23,680
So we actually have another equilibrium

263
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:25,120
at the other extreme.

264
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:28,280
That's also totally an equilibrium.

265
00:10:28,280 --> 00:10:30,200
So then you would sit back and say, all right,

266
00:10:30,200 --> 00:10:31,600
well, what extra assumptions do we

267
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:34,160
need in order for this to make sense?

268
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:37,000
So one thing you might do is, what

269
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,280
would be a sub-game perfection, where, sure, the customer's

270
00:10:40,280 --> 00:10:41,760
going to say we won't pay anything more than marginal

271
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:42,280
cost.

272
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:43,920
But when you show up at a store and they're

273
00:10:43,920 --> 00:10:45,280
charging more than marginal cost,

274
00:10:45,280 --> 00:10:47,640
what does the monopolist think you're really going to do?

275
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:48,280
Buy it.

276
00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:49,440
Buy it.

277
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:52,280
So you could say, really, sub-game perfection

278
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:55,560
is built into the standard monopoly model, a concept

279
00:10:55,560 --> 00:10:57,760
that you may have never heard of until last week.

280
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:01,200
Actually underlies the whole ordinary store monopoly.

281
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:03,760
Or you might actually say maybe what's really

282
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:05,320
going on is a reputational model,

283
00:11:05,320 --> 00:11:08,080
where the monopolist is trying to build up a reputation

284
00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:11,000
for charging the monopoly price.

285
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:12,720
And it's hard, on the other hand,

286
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:16,120
for the customers to build up a reputation for not doing that.

287
00:11:16,120 --> 00:11:17,920
So customers say, come on, you guys.

288
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:19,800
Let's all build up a reputation for not

289
00:11:19,800 --> 00:11:22,240
being willing to go along with these store prices.

290
00:11:22,240 --> 00:11:24,520
But of course, every individual is likely to say, well,

291
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:26,960
I'd like to go and eat some chicken this weekend.

292
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:28,640
If I just go and pay the monopoly price,

293
00:11:28,640 --> 00:11:29,760
I can get chicken.

294
00:11:29,760 --> 00:11:31,560
Whereas the monopolist, on the other hand,

295
00:11:31,560 --> 00:11:33,760
doesn't have to go and get a whole group of people

296
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:35,400
together to go along with this plan.

297
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:37,560
Just says, look, this is what I'm doing.

298
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:40,440
And every week, I charge this price for chickens.

299
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:42,080
I've been doing this for a long time.

300
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:43,880
So if you think I'm going to cut the price,

301
00:11:43,880 --> 00:11:45,280
you're sadly mistaken, people.

302
00:11:45,280 --> 00:11:47,720
So that might be it.

303
00:11:47,720 --> 00:11:50,680
So in any case, the simple monopoly model

304
00:11:50,680 --> 00:11:53,800
actually needs better intellectual foundations

305
00:11:53,800 --> 00:11:56,960
than your intro textbook gives it.

306
00:11:56,960 --> 00:11:59,840
But of course, that doesn't mean that it's wrong.

307
00:11:59,840 --> 00:12:02,160
So it means that, yeah, so you need extra foundations.

308
00:12:02,160 --> 00:12:04,720
But you just figure out whatever foundations are necessary

309
00:12:04,720 --> 00:12:06,080
to make the standard story right,

310
00:12:06,080 --> 00:12:08,560
because the standard story does make sense.

311
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:11,440
So intuitively, the standard story makes a lot of sense.

312
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:14,480
Again, especially if we are thinking about the simplest

313
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:17,400
kind of monopoly, which is where the government goes

314
00:12:17,400 --> 00:12:19,680
into an industry where there's no real reason for there

315
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:20,360
to be a monopoly.

316
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:22,760
And then they give a piece of paper to one firm that says,

317
00:12:22,760 --> 00:12:24,680
you are the only one allowed to produce this.

318
00:12:24,680 --> 00:12:27,560
And then they jail anyone who tries to meet with them.

319
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:29,760
So that is the simplest case.

320
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:32,880
Notice here how I drew a flat marginal cost curve.

321
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:34,280
In the real world, how often do you

322
00:12:34,280 --> 00:12:36,160
get a monopolist in an industry where there's

323
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:38,320
a flat marginal cost curve?

324
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:40,840
Pretty much never, right?

325
00:12:40,840 --> 00:12:44,560
So instead, the only way you get monopoly

326
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:46,480
in an industry with a flat marginal cost curve

327
00:12:46,480 --> 00:12:49,520
is something like agri-oil, like when their government organized

328
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:50,840
agricultural cartels.

329
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:53,000
Again, why did the government organize the cartels?

330
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:54,960
Well, because there's very flat marginal cost.

331
00:12:54,960 --> 00:12:57,520
So if the government just looked the other way

332
00:12:57,520 --> 00:13:00,360
while the firm is trying to collude, it falls through.

333
00:13:00,360 --> 00:13:02,760
It's too easy for there to be more entry,

334
00:13:02,760 --> 00:13:06,000
too easy for one old apple to gain production.

335
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:07,440
So that's not likely to work.

336
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:10,120
But anyway, if we think about the simple case where

337
00:13:10,120 --> 00:13:12,680
it is a government-sponsored monopoly, where there's

338
00:13:12,680 --> 00:13:15,640
no possible economic reason for it to exist,

339
00:13:15,640 --> 00:13:18,160
other than the government wants to go and help out

340
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:21,000
the bully in the dictator, something like that.

341
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,080
Anyway, so in that very simple case,

342
00:13:23,080 --> 00:13:25,640
this seems like a fine model of what's likely to happen.

343
00:13:29,760 --> 00:13:32,960
But for all other cases, when it's not just government

344
00:13:32,960 --> 00:13:34,800
going and handing out a piece of paper saying,

345
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:36,920
only you can legally prove something,

346
00:13:36,920 --> 00:13:38,960
then there is a big real-world problem,

347
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:41,320
which is the problem of potential competition.

348
00:13:45,480 --> 00:13:47,440
Something can literally be a monopoly

349
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:49,040
where there's only one seller, right?

350
00:13:49,040 --> 00:13:51,680
Like some mono meaning one, poly meaning seller,

351
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:53,800
or something like that.

352
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:55,200
Something like polis or something.

353
00:13:55,200 --> 00:13:56,880
So that's the original word.

354
00:13:56,880 --> 00:13:59,320
There can literally only be one seller of something.

355
00:13:59,320 --> 00:14:02,320
And yet, they might actually act in a way

356
00:14:02,320 --> 00:14:05,120
that's very different from this if they are worried

357
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:07,280
that they won't remain the only firm if they

358
00:14:07,280 --> 00:14:09,040
change their behavior.

359
00:14:09,040 --> 00:14:11,560
And it's a pretty simple point.

360
00:14:11,560 --> 00:14:14,960
So how can we go and model potential competition?

361
00:14:14,960 --> 00:14:18,240
How can we go and amend the regular monopoly model

362
00:14:18,240 --> 00:14:22,800
to take into account the possibility of potential entry

363
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:25,200
or potential competition?

364
00:14:25,200 --> 00:14:28,080
By the way, again, this is something where, like in 1990,

365
00:14:28,080 --> 00:14:30,240
you said this thing, a lot of people would still say,

366
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:32,960
ah, come on, potential competition, no.

367
00:14:32,960 --> 00:14:35,440
In the internet age, I think it's almost crazy

368
00:14:35,440 --> 00:14:38,000
to go and dismiss potential competition

369
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:39,800
because if you don't believe in that,

370
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:42,840
what's your story for why Amazon doesn't raise prices?

371
00:14:42,840 --> 00:14:46,520
We think that Amazon would remain the only Amazon

372
00:14:46,520 --> 00:14:47,640
if they doubled its prices.

373
00:14:47,640 --> 00:14:49,480
Why would they double them?

374
00:14:49,480 --> 00:14:53,240
We really think the demand elasticity is that high

375
00:14:53,240 --> 00:14:55,680
that Amazon would actually lose money if they doubled

376
00:14:55,680 --> 00:14:57,840
their prices today.

377
00:14:57,840 --> 00:14:59,960
Seems like they could totally get away with it.

378
00:14:59,960 --> 00:15:01,560
And there's plenty of other firms

379
00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:03,480
that basically work the same way, right?

380
00:15:03,480 --> 00:15:06,120
Plenty of other famous internet firms like Netflix.

381
00:15:06,120 --> 00:15:09,720
So if Netflix were to suddenly greatly increase its prices,

382
00:15:09,720 --> 00:15:12,320
that's why I'm not quite as sure that they don't have high demand

383
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:12,800
elasticity.

384
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:15,640
But it sort of seems like they could get away with more.

385
00:15:15,640 --> 00:15:19,960
But Netflix probably is worried about making more Netflixes.

386
00:15:19,960 --> 00:15:22,280
They don't want there to be other competitors.

387
00:15:22,280 --> 00:15:24,880
And that is a reason to constantly be producing

388
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:29,120
more stuff and to keep prices at a reasonable level

389
00:15:29,120 --> 00:15:31,680
so that customers are so happy they aren't looking around

390
00:15:31,680 --> 00:15:32,920
for anything else.

391
00:15:32,920 --> 00:15:34,920
So basically, offer a better feel

392
00:15:34,920 --> 00:15:37,880
than anyone else on Earth thinks they can make a profit at.

393
00:15:37,880 --> 00:15:41,920
And then you've got a winning business model.

394
00:15:41,920 --> 00:15:44,240
All right, so what can we do?

395
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:46,000
Well, there's several different approaches

396
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:47,320
to potential competition.

397
00:15:47,320 --> 00:15:50,440
Let's start with contestability.

398
00:15:56,760 --> 00:16:00,880
Contestability was a model that became big during the late 70s

399
00:16:00,880 --> 00:16:04,160
when economists were talking about deregulating airlines.

400
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:06,720
And there was the idea of, look, even if there's only one

401
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:10,000
airline servicing a certain regional airport,

402
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:11,840
there's a lot of airlines in the world.

403
00:16:11,840 --> 00:16:15,080
So if the one airline that's there starts raising its prices,

404
00:16:15,080 --> 00:16:16,360
this will go into track entry.

405
00:16:16,360 --> 00:16:20,320
And that will prevent them from doing so.

406
00:16:20,320 --> 00:16:24,000
So subsequently, we pointed out this is probably

407
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:29,280
exaggerated, although the basic point of if an airline is

408
00:16:29,280 --> 00:16:30,960
permanently really profitable in an area,

409
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:33,200
then they're likely to go and get some other firm that

410
00:16:33,200 --> 00:16:33,960
wants to go there.

411
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:35,440
But it may not happen that quickly.

412
00:16:35,440 --> 00:16:37,400
People put a lot of points about how planes can

413
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:39,080
move around really easily.

414
00:16:39,080 --> 00:16:41,360
So yeah, you can move the planes around really easily.

415
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:42,840
But you're working on the contracts

416
00:16:42,840 --> 00:16:46,800
to get access to the terminal and things like that.

417
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:49,000
That's probably not going to happen.

418
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:53,320
Anyway, so the basic set of contestability models is this.

419
00:16:53,320 --> 00:16:57,160
First, you've got an incumbent firm that sets its price.

420
00:16:57,160 --> 00:16:58,640
So normally, you call it P sub i.

421
00:16:58,640 --> 00:17:01,200
So that's the price of the incumbent.

422
00:17:01,200 --> 00:17:03,440
And then a potential entrant looks at the mark

423
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:06,560
and decides whether he wants to enter.

424
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:09,200
And if he enters, then the entrant sets the price.

425
00:17:09,200 --> 00:17:10,480
And then customers are scammed.

426
00:17:10,480 --> 00:17:13,240
And if he does enter, then his price is called PE.

427
00:17:13,240 --> 00:17:15,400
And then consumers just buy from whoever

428
00:17:15,400 --> 00:17:17,360
sells at the cheaper price.

429
00:17:17,360 --> 00:17:18,760
Simple point.

430
00:17:18,760 --> 00:17:19,760
OK.

431
00:17:19,760 --> 00:17:21,360
All right, so what's going to happen?

432
00:17:21,360 --> 00:17:23,280
Well, suppose that we got total cost is just

433
00:17:23,280 --> 00:17:27,920
equal to B times quantity.

434
00:17:27,920 --> 00:17:29,440
And then we can set this up.

435
00:17:29,440 --> 00:17:32,600
So if total cost is just B times quantity,

436
00:17:32,600 --> 00:17:35,000
then marginal cost looks like what?

437
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:39,400
What is marginal cost if total cost equals BQ?

438
00:17:39,400 --> 00:17:42,960
The partial derivative of cost with respect to Q, which is?

439
00:17:42,960 --> 00:17:43,480
B.

440
00:17:43,480 --> 00:17:43,980
B.

441
00:17:43,980 --> 00:17:46,440
So not that.

442
00:17:46,440 --> 00:17:47,560
That total cost.

443
00:17:47,560 --> 00:17:48,400
Yes, yes, yes.

444
00:17:48,400 --> 00:17:50,040
Yeah, total cost is that.

445
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:51,480
But the derivative is that.

446
00:17:51,480 --> 00:17:52,480
Yes.

447
00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:55,120
Yes, so B. So that's your partial cost.

448
00:17:55,120 --> 00:17:58,840
Partial cost equals B. All right.

449
00:17:58,840 --> 00:18:00,880
OK, so what's going to happen?

450
00:18:00,880 --> 00:18:04,520
Well, so suppose that the incumbent sets a price that

451
00:18:04,520 --> 00:18:07,040
is greater than B. All right.

452
00:18:07,040 --> 00:18:10,400
So if the incumbent sets a price greater than B,

453
00:18:10,400 --> 00:18:12,680
then the entrant will come and take a look.

454
00:18:12,680 --> 00:18:15,400
And he will see, here's the man curve here.

455
00:18:15,400 --> 00:18:17,800
All right, so if the incumbent goes and sets a price here

456
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:19,920
greater than B, so that's PI.

457
00:18:19,920 --> 00:18:22,920
Now, if you're the entrant, you have a really easy strategy

458
00:18:22,920 --> 00:18:25,920
that you can apply, which will make you money for sure.

459
00:18:25,920 --> 00:18:28,840
And well, so he's charging PI.

460
00:18:28,840 --> 00:18:30,120
What do you want to charge?

461
00:18:30,120 --> 00:18:30,620
P.

462
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:31,120
Yes, I know.

463
00:18:31,120 --> 00:18:32,040
I have PI.

464
00:18:32,040 --> 00:18:37,080
So PE equals PI minus an epsilon, minus a penny.

465
00:18:37,080 --> 00:18:39,640
Charge the price one penny below the incumbent.

466
00:18:39,640 --> 00:18:42,960
And then the result is that you steal the entire market

467
00:18:42,960 --> 00:18:44,680
from him.

468
00:18:44,680 --> 00:18:48,000
And the incumbent gets no money.

469
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:49,640
All right, so if the incumbent realizes

470
00:18:49,640 --> 00:18:51,160
this is what's going to happen, then he

471
00:18:51,160 --> 00:18:53,240
must find out and charge a lower price than that.

472
00:18:53,240 --> 00:18:54,960
But then the entrant can go and charge

473
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:57,200
a price an epsilon below that.

474
00:18:57,200 --> 00:19:00,600
So if you think it through, then you say, all right, well,

475
00:19:00,600 --> 00:19:01,160
so let's see.

476
00:19:01,160 --> 00:19:03,400
It's like, nope, the incumbent can never charge a price

477
00:19:03,400 --> 00:19:04,440
over marginal cost.

478
00:19:04,440 --> 00:19:07,000
Would the incumbent ever charge a price

479
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:08,600
that's less than marginal cost?

480
00:19:08,600 --> 00:19:09,480
That's idiotic, right?

481
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:11,100
You're just losing money on everything.

482
00:19:11,100 --> 00:19:13,680
Yes, you do get to run the market, but you lose money.

483
00:19:13,680 --> 00:19:15,880
So if he's never going to charge a price over marginal cost,

484
00:19:15,880 --> 00:19:17,760
never going to charge a price under marginal cost,

485
00:19:17,760 --> 00:19:19,640
how about a charge of price exactly equal to marginal cost?

486
00:19:19,640 --> 00:19:21,440
Is that a possible equilibrium?

487
00:19:21,440 --> 00:19:23,080
And the answer is it is.

488
00:19:23,080 --> 00:19:25,840
So the incumbent charges a price equal to marginal cost.

489
00:19:25,840 --> 00:19:28,400
And then this does not provoke any entry.

490
00:19:28,400 --> 00:19:30,600
And so the incumbent is a monopolist,

491
00:19:30,600 --> 00:19:33,680
but he's a monopolist who acts exactly like a perfect event

492
00:19:33,680 --> 00:19:35,160
here.

493
00:19:35,160 --> 00:19:38,160
And that is the amazing result of the simple contestability

494
00:19:38,160 --> 00:19:39,520
model.

495
00:19:39,520 --> 00:19:42,280
In fact, the only Nash equilibrium

496
00:19:42,280 --> 00:19:45,520
is one where the incumbent charges a price equal to b,

497
00:19:45,520 --> 00:19:47,200
and then there's no entry.

498
00:19:47,200 --> 00:19:48,120
So there it is.

499
00:19:50,960 --> 00:19:52,320
Now, what's so amazing about this?

500
00:19:52,320 --> 00:19:55,400
Well, did anyone ever have, say, an intermediate microeconomics

501
00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:57,680
textbook that says that markets fail

502
00:19:57,680 --> 00:19:59,960
unless there's perfect combination?

503
00:19:59,960 --> 00:20:00,520
Did anyone have one?


